1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,450
This log measures three hundred
millimeters across its small end.

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,450
What is the biggest square
beam you could cut from it?

3
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:12,923
A Japanese carpenter doesn't
reach for a calculator.

4
00:00:13,373 --> 00:00:15,183
They turn their square over.

5
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:25,717
This is a sashigane, the
Japanese carpenter's square.

6
00:00:26,227 --> 00:00:29,327
It's a thin L of steel, today usually

7
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:32,837
stainless, marked as a
ruler along its edges.

8
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:35,803
The long arm is the nagate.

9
00:00:36,353 --> 00:00:38,483
The short arm is the tsumate.

10
00:00:39,193 --> 00:00:43,093
The common size is scaled to
five hundred millimeters on

11
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:46,423
the long arm, and two
hundred fifty on the short.

12
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:50,063
Beside it, the Western framing square:

13
00:00:50,103 --> 00:00:54,223
twenty-four inches by sixteen,
with a two-inch blade.

14
00:00:55,213 --> 00:00:58,083
Both are squares and rulers at once.

15
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:03,153
But the sashigane is only
about fifteen millimeters wide,

16
00:01:03,193 --> 00:01:06,903
and only about one and a
half millimeters thick.

17
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:11,243
That thinness is deliberate.

18
00:01:11,813 --> 00:01:17,793
The blade flexes and springs back, so the
carpenter can bend it slightly with their

19
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:22,383
fingers and press it flat against
the wood, right where the line goes.

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,830
Used as a square, it carried the lines
across the posts in the last episode.

21
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:30,950
How to hold it?

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,570
That comes next time, with
the rest of the marking kit.

23
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,770
The front carries ordinary
measurements, the omote-me.

24
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,950
Today that's usually millimeters.

25
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,050
Some squares carry the
traditional shaku units instead.

26
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:53,050
Turn it over, and the back carries
a second scale, the ura-me.

27
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:55,490
Its marks are spaced wider.

28
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:00,640
Each unit is a front unit
times the square root of two:

29
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:03,284
about one point four one four.

30
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:10,490
Why would anyone want a stretched ruler?

31
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:14,670
Because of one shape that
is everywhere in timber:

32
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,100
the square.

33
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:22,144
A square's diagonal is always its
side times the square root of two.

34
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:29,037
So measure a diagonal with the back scale,
and the number you read is the side.

35
00:02:29,927 --> 00:02:33,867
A square one hundred
millimeters on a side has a

36
00:02:33,907 --> 00:02:37,377
diagonal of one hundred
forty-one point four.

37
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:41,769
On the back scale, that same
length reads one hundred.

38
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:47,897
Back to the log.

39
00:02:48,507 --> 00:02:54,837
Measure at the small end, because a log
tapers and the small end limits the beam.

40
00:02:55,567 --> 00:02:59,427
The biggest square you can
cut there has its corners on

41
00:02:59,467 --> 00:03:03,437
the circle, so its diagonal
is the log's diameter.

42
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:07,627
Lay the back scale across
the small end, straight

43
00:03:07,667 --> 00:03:11,237
through the center, and
read it at the far edge.

44
00:03:11,347 --> 00:03:13,397
Two hundred twelve.

45
00:03:13,867 --> 00:03:18,204
That's the side of the largest
square beam this log can give:

46
00:03:18,244 --> 00:03:20,457
two hundred twelve millimeters.

47
00:03:20,933 --> 00:03:27,143
No calculator, no pencil arithmetic,
no chance to slip a digit.

48
00:03:27,873 --> 00:03:30,583
The arithmetic is built into the tool.

49
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:41,830
The same scale is used in laying out
roofs, where hips run on diagonals.

50
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:48,150
That craft has its own name,
kikujutsu, and it's beyond this series.

51
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,930
Some squares add one
more scale, the marume.

52
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:57,170
Its units are a front unit divided by pi.

53
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:01,770
Lay it across a log's diameter,
and it reads the circumference.

54
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:08,390
The framing square plays the
same trick in its own way.

55
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:13,690
Its rafter and board-measure tables
put the arithmetic on the steel too.

56
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:16,970
Two traditions, one idea:

57
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,250
let the tool do the math.

58
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:23,210
Next time, the rest of the marking kit:

59
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:28,800
the ink line and the ink pen,
and the gauges that scribe

60
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:32,970
parallel lines, each
beside its Western cousin.

61
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:38,490
A thin, springy square
that is also a calculator.

62
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:40,950
The front measures lengths.

63
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:45,230
The back measures diagonals,
and reads you the side.
